<para id="RM00000160F01TX" category="C" type-id="3031K" name-id="0659S-01" from="200912">
<dtccode>P0031/21</dtccode>
<dtcname>O2 (A/F) Sensor Heater Performance DeteriorationB1S1</dtcname>
<dtccode>P0032/21</dtccode>
<dtcname>O2 (A/F) Sensor Heater OvercurrentB1S1</dtcname>
<subpara id="RM00000160F01TX_01" type-id="31" category="03" proc-id="RM0550J___00002TU00001">
<name>Circuit Description</name>
<content5 releasenbr="1">
<list1 type="nonmark">
<item>
<ptxt>A three-way catalytic converter is used to efficiently purify the CO (carbon monoxide), HC (hydrocarbons) and NOx (nitrogen oxides) in the exhaust gas. This three-way catalytic converter functions most efficiently near the stoichiometric air-fuel ratio. For this reason, CO, HC and NOx cannot be efficiently purified unless the engine is accurately controlled near the stoichiometric air-fuel ratio. The air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) is installed for the purpose of this control. The air fuel ratio sensor outputs a voltage (*) roughly proportional to the air-fuel ratio, and the engine control computer controls the air-fuel ratio based on that voltage.</ptxt>
<ptxt>A zirconium element is used for the sensor element. The inside of the zirconium element is exposed to the outside air and the outside is exposed to the exhaust gas, and it generates a voltage according to the difference in oxygen concentration between the exhaust gas and the outside air. When the exhaust temperature is low, the sensor cannot generate a useful voltage signal without supplementary heating. For this reason, the air fuel ratio sensor has a heater that warms the zirconia element, and the engine control computer regulates the heater current by duty ratio control.</ptxt>
<ptxt>*: The value changes within the engine control computer. Because the air fuel ratio sensor has a current output, a constant voltage is displayed when measured by the engine control computer.</ptxt>
<figure>
<graphic graphicname="A173183J09" width="7.106578999in" height="3.779676365in"/>
</figure>
</item>
</list1>
